                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

The domestic hot water system for your house is integrated into the boiler, so please see my upgrade recommendation &amp; energy/cost savings within the previous section about the Heating System.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 85.7% AFUE oil boiler that appears in good functioning order and this same boiler heats your domestic hot water year round via an indirect zoned loop. The estimated hot water annual efficiency is 0.60 Energy Factor (EF).



As we discussed at your house, over 98% of all new housing constructed in Yellowknife in the past 8 years has chosen to install high efficiency (95% AFUE minimum) propane boilers or furnaces ........ and the propane suppliers and local heating contractors have a good "handle" on how to manage this high efficiency equipment in our cold &amp; long winters.



For your longer term planning, I have modeled the option for you to upgrade to a 95% AFUE propane boiler that will both heat your house and heat your domestic hot water (now up to a 0.82 EF).



Savings: By replacing your oil boiler with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing propane "combi" boiler, you could potentially save $ 1,420 per year in energy costs ($1,281 in heating savings &amp; $139 in hot water savings) , decrease your annual energy usage by 29.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. In addition, you may enjoy some savings on your property insurance by not having an oil tank on your property.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Slab Foundation - Front Section of Secondary Suite



We had discussed that your house is mostly insulated to a high or reasonable level (R64 in attic, R20 exterior walls, interior of crawlspace concrete walls are insulated), however the floor of the front section of the "secondary suite" may just be an uninsulated 2x10 false floor ?? There was no way for me to access this floor area to validate this, however you confirmed that it likely is an uninsulated void. There is carpet on the floor that helps to insulate it a bit.



Just for the fun of it, I modeled adding an R28 batt to that 2x10 floor cavity.



Savings:

By adding R28 the floor of the front section of the secondary suite, you could potentially save $ 142 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Flourescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (both kitchen range fan, both bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 18.1 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler is a direct-vent design &amp; is not susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - There are 2 x Lifebreath HRV's - one for each living unit

        - 2 x kitchen range hood fans - one for each living unit 

        - 2 x bathroom fans - one in each living unit (I am not counting the one in the attached &amp; heated garage)

        - A clothes dryer venting to the outside



You should consider replacing the bathroom exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR rated fan that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $0.68 per litre

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



